[38;5;252m[1m## What I found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne real translation bug class.[22m The C uses [38;5;10mSPX_VLA[0m variable-length arrays, so its buffers grow to whatever length the caller asks for. The Rust translation had sized them for the library's [3minternal[23m worst case only. Since these are all exported symbols, an external caller passing a larger argument got a Rust panic (process abort) where C returned a correct result:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mSPX_thash[0m — [38;5;10minblocks > max(SPX_WOTS_LEN, SPX_FORS_TREES)[0m[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mSPX_blake256_mgf1[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_blake512_mgf1[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_mgf1_256[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_mgf1_512[0m — [38;5;10minlen[0m above the internal maximum[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mSPX_treehash[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_wots_treehashx1[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_fors_treehashx1[0m — [38;5;10mtree_height > max(SPX_TREE_HEIGHT, SPX_FORS_HEIGHT)[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fixed with a new [38;5;10msrc/vla.rs[0m ([38;5;10mVla[0m/[38;5;10mVlaU32[0m): an inline buffer at the old fixed capacity, so nothing is allocated on any internal call path, with a heap fallback above it. All affected sites in the four backends' [38;5;10mthash[0m, both [38;5;10mmgf1[0m pairs, [38;5;10mutils.rs[0m and [38;5;10mutilsx1.rs[0m now accept arbitrary lengths and match C byte-for-byte.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Everything else was already faithful, including the deliberate C quirks — [38;5;10mblake[0m's [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m writing 32/64 bytes into an [38;5;10mSPX_N[0m output, [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m filling [38;5;10m2*SPX_N + 32[0m bytes but hashing only [38;5;10mSPX_N + 32[0m, [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_open[0m zeroing [38;5;10msmlen[0m (not [38;5;10mmlen[0m) bytes on rejection, and [38;5;10mfors_treehashx1[0m being declared with [38;5;10mleaf_info_x1*[0m while actually receiving a [38;5;10mfors_gen_leaf_info*[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
